{
  "global_card_id": "trend_line",
  "global_canonical": "Trend Line",
  "type": "merged_concept",
  "school": "Price Action",
  "aliases": [
    "Trendline"
  ],
  "definition": "A diagonal line drawn on a price chart by connecting at least two consecutive swing lows (uptrend) or swing highs (downtrend) to visualize trend direction, slope, and phase. It acts as dynamic support or resistance, can outline chart patterns and channels, and helps identify rejection, breakout, and reversal points. A line break signals a possible change in trend direction, though confirmation methods vary by approach.",
  "definition_per_source": {
    "SMC-Strong-Weak-Supplement": "A straight line connecting at least two consecutive swing lows (uptrend) or swing highs (downtrend) to identify trend direction and slope, acting as dynamic support or resistance and often forming the boundaries of chart patterns such as triangles.",
    "SMC-Supplement-PLb0LJvd-CX1wZ": "A line connecting swing highs or swing lows in a trending market, acting as dynamic support or resistance. In an uptrend it connects swing lows along the bottom; in a downtrend it connects swing highs along the top. Breaking a trend line can signal a possible trend direction change.",
    "SMC-Supplement-PLv-X125": "A diagonal line drawn across consecutive swing highs or swing lows (or on the RSI indicator) to visualize trend direction—uptrend, downtrend, or consolidation. Built by connecting the most-touched points, it identifies rejection, breakout, and reversal points. A valid break requires a candle close beyond the line, not merely a wick through it."
  },
  "identification_rules": [
    "For an uptrend, connect at least two consecutive higher swing lows along the bottom of price movement; price should remain above the line while the trend is intact.",
    "For a downtrend, connect at least two consecutive lower swing highs along the top of price movement; price should remain below the line while the trend is intact.",
    "Confirm validity through multiple touches (bounces); PLv-X125 requires price to respect the line at least twice, with wicks or bodies aligning without significant penetration through the middle of candles.",
    "Maximize the number of touch points and prioritize obvious, easy-to-spot trend lines, since more traders watch them (PLb0LJvd and PLv-X125 both emphasize maximizing touches).",
    "On whether to draw through wicks or bodies: PLb0LJvd says it does not matter as long as touches are maximized, while the Strong-Weak source says avoid significantly cutting through candle bodies.",
    "Treat the trend line as an area/zone rather than a precise solid line, similar to support and resistance zones (PLb0LJvd).",
    "Do not force a trend line onto the chart; use it as guidance for overall analysis and only draw once a clear pattern of higher lows or lower highs is established (PLb0LJvd, PLv-X125).",
    "Adjust and extend the trend line as new swings form over time, stretching it beyond the last touch in case price returns (PLb0LJvd).",
    "Confirm the line aligns with market structure: higher highs/higher lows for an uptrend, lower highs/lower lows for a downtrend (PLv-X125).",
    "A valid break occurs only when a candle closes beyond the line, not merely a wick through it; wait for the breakout candle to close (PLv-X125). Occasional wicks beyond may be liquidity traps and do not invalidate the line if structure holds.",
    "When price moves sideways between an uptrend line and a downtrend line, label the phase as consolidation (PLv-X125).",
    "To form a trend channel, draw a parallel line on the opposite side connecting the corresponding swing highs or lows to enclose price action (Strong-Weak, PLv-X125).",
    "Trend lines can outline chart patterns by connecting price extremes, e.g., the upper and lower boundaries of triangles (Strong-Weak).",
    "Trend lines can also be drawn on the RSI indicator: connect two or more consecutive RSI swing lows (uptrend) or swing highs (downtrend); an RSI break occurs when RSI closes beyond the diagonal in the opposite direction (PLv-X125).",
    "Trend lines are most reliable during high-volume sessions (London through New York close) where market structure is most consistent (PLv-X125)."
  ],
  "common_mistakes": [
    "Entering solely because price touched a trend line without waiting for price action confirmation signals (PLb0LJvd).",
    "Entering solely because a trend line broke without combining it with other price action techniques (PLb0LJvd).",
    "Forcing a trend line onto the chart when one does not naturally fit the price structure (PLb0LJvd), or drawing it before a clear pattern of lower highs/higher lows is established (PLv-X125).",
    "Treating a trend line as a precise solid line rather than as a zone or area (PLb0LJvd).",
    "Obsessing over whether to draw through wicks or candle closes instead of maximizing the number of touches (PLb0LJvd).",
    "Entering before the candle closes beyond the line, risking a false breakout where price wicks through but reverses (PLv-X125).",
    "Mistaking liquidity-trapping wicks beyond the line for genuine breakouts, causing premature entries or exits (PLv-X125).",
    "Trading trend lines during choppy, sideways ranges instead of waiting for clear trending structure (PLv-X125).",
    "Trading during low-volume periods or outside the London/New York sessions where trend lines are less reliable (PLv-X125).",
    "Using trend lines in isolation without confirming with market structure, divergences, Break of Structure, or Fibonacci retracement (PLv-X125; aligns with PLb0LJvd).",
    "Assuming a trend line break automatically means trend reversal, when price can still maintain its overall HH/HL or LH/LL pattern despite breaking the diagonal (PLv-X125).",
    "Confusing a trend line touch with a Break of Structure — the rejection must produce a BOS before applying further entry tools (PLv-X125).",
    "Not drawing trend lines on the RSI itself, missing momentum trend shifts visible on the indicator (PLv-X125).",
    "Ignoring RSI range context — breaking a downtrend while RSI is in the upper range (70+) signals strong momentum, not a sell opportunity; and not waiting for divergences within the 70/30 range before acting (PLv-X125).",
    "Looking for the wrong divergence type relative to trend — in an uptrend along a bullish line, look for hidden bullish divergences, not regular ones (PLv-X125).",
    "Ignoring low-volume wicks when drawing trend lines on channels; measure to roughly half the wick or the channel boundary rather than the extreme of an outsized wick (PLv-X125)."
  ],
  "trading_implication": "Across all sources, trend lines act as dynamic support/resistance where price tends to react, define directional bias, and signal potential reversals or acceleration when broken. Strong-Weak treats a decisive break below an uptrend line or above a downtrend line as a reversal/acceleration signal prompting entry or exit. PLb0LJvd stresses never trading a trend line in isolation: entries require price action confirmation and confluence with other factors (e.g., support-turned-resistance), where convergence zones offer high-probability setups. PLv-X125 uses trend lines for entries, stop-loss trailing, and confluence filtering—rejections give continuation entries while breaks give reversal signals—combined with RSI divergences (regular for reversals, hidden for continuations), Break of Structure, and Fibonacci 0.5–0.618 gold zone; RSI trend line breaks can give early momentum-shift confirmation, with targets set at prior swing points.",
  "merge_notes": "Key cross-source differences: (1) Break confirmation — PLv-X125 strictly requires a candle close beyond the line, while Strong-Weak refers to a generic 'decisive break' and PLb0LJvd warns against trading any break without further confirmation. (2) Drawing precision — PLb0LJvd says wick-vs-body does not matter as long as touches are maximized, while Strong-Weak says avoid cutting through candle bodies. (3) PLv-X125 uniquely extends trend lines to the RSI indicator and integrates session-timing, divergence, BOS, and Fibonacci confluence, whereas the other two keep the concept to raw price action.",
